Title: Meaning of "conditional uncorrelatedness"

Suppose that $(\Omega,F,P)$ is a probability space, $H$ a sub-sigma-algebra and $X$ and $Y$ two random variables. ChatGPT claims that $X$ and $Y$ are said to be conditionally uncorrelated if
\begin{equation}\tag{1}
\operatorname{E}(XY\mid H)=\operatorname{E}(X\mid H)\operatorname{E}(Y\mid H).
\end{equation}
However, I would have expected that the actual standard meaning of "conditionally uncorrelated" is
\begin{equation}\tag{2}
\forall_{A\in H:P(A)>0}\operatorname{E}(XY\mid A)=\operatorname{E}(X\mid A)\operatorname{E}(Y\mid A)
\end{equation}
It seems to me that $(1)$ and $(2)$ are not equivalent and in fact I couldn't even show that at least one property follows from the other.

That being said, assuming $(1)$ would have simplified a proof for me (I have some freedom on how to model the problem that I am working on), but I feel like $(1)$ does not have any intuitive meaning, whereas $(2)$ seems like an assumption that could be justified when modeling a real-world problem.

So, what is the consensus of the mathematical community? Am I right that $(2)$ is a common property, whereas $(1)$ is rather meaningless?

Title: Proving an identity for a semi-orthogonal matrix

$
\def\R#1{{\mathbb R}^{#1}}
\def\T{{\sf T}}
\def\a{\alpha}   \def\b{\beta}
\def\t{\times}   \def\emdash{\,–}
\def\o{{\tt1}}
\def\LR#1{\left(#1\right)}
\def\BR#1{\left[#1\right]}
\def\CR#1{\left\lbrace #1 \right\rbrace}
\def\op#1{\operatorname{#1}}
\def\trace#1{\op{\sf tr}\LR{#1}}
\def\frob#1{\left\| #1 \right\|_F}
\def\q{\quad}      \def\qq{\qquad}
\def\qif{\q\iff\q} \def\qiq{\q\implies\q} \def\where{\q{\sf where}\q}
\def\m#1{\left[\begin{array}{r}#1\end{array}\right]}
\def\mb#1{\left[\begin{array}{c|c}#1\end{array}\right]}
\def\green#1{\color{lime}{#1}}   \def\GLR#1{\green{\LR{#1}}}
\def\red#1{\color{red}{#1}}      \def\RLR#1{\red{\LR{#1}}}
\def\fracLR#1#2{\LR{\frac{#1}{#2}}}
$Let vector $n\in\R 3$ be the unit normal to a tangent plane defined by
the vectors $\CR{a,b}$.

Given the following matrix variables
$$\eqalign{
R &= \mb{a&b&n} \where \;\,R^\T R = I_3 = RR^\T \\
B &= \mb{a&b}   \q\;\;\where \;\,B^\T B = I_2 \ne BB^\T \\
H &= H^\T,\q H^{-1}\!H = I_3 \\
}$$
$$\eqalign{
M &= \LR{H - HB\LR{B^\T HB}^{-1}B^\T H} = M^\T \qq\q\;\; \\
}$$
Multiplication by $B^\T/B$ reveals that $M$ is a rank-one matrix
$$\eqalign{
B^\T M =0,\q MB= 0 \qiq M = \mu\:nn^\T \qiq \mu=n^\T Mn \\
}$$

I'd like to show that
$$\eqalign{
\mu^{-1} \;=\; n^\T H^{-1}n \\
}$$
I've been trying to find an alternate expression for $M \LR{{\rm or}\ \LR{B^\T HB}^{-1}}$ in terms of $H^{-1}$ via clever application of the Sherman-Morrison formula, but I seem to be going in circles. It feels like I'm not taking full advantage of the semi-orthogonal property of $B$.

Any ideas? Thanks.
